# Jetpack Compose — UI

Full conventions: @lanes/kotlin-compose/CONVENTIONS.md

## The core principle

**The UI is dumb.** Composables render state and forward actions. Nothing else. Zero business
logic, zero data transformation, minimal side effects. All state lives in the ViewModel.

## The Root / Screen split

Every screen is two composables:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun ProfileRoot(viewModel: ProfileViewModel = koinViewModel()) {
    val state by viewModel.state.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()
    ProfileScreen(state = state, onAction = viewModel::onAction)
}

@Composable
fun ProfileScreen(state: ProfileState, onAction: (ProfileAction) -> Unit) { /* pure UI */ }
```

`Root` is stateful and knows the ViewModel. `Screen` is pure, takes state and a single `onAction`
lambda, and is fully previewable and testable. **Never call a ViewModel from inside `Screen`.**

## State hoisting

- A composable that owns state cannot be reused. Hoist to the lowest common ancestor that needs it.
- `remember { }` for values that survive recomposition; `rememberSaveable { }` for values that must
  survive configuration change and process death.
- `derivedStateOf` when a value is computed from other state and changes **less often** than its
  inputs. Using it everywhere costs more than it saves.

## Side effects — pick the right one

| Need | Use |
|---|---|
| Run on enter / on key change, suspend-capable | `LaunchedEffect(key)` |
| Clean up when leaving composition | `DisposableEffect(key)` |
| Read latest value inside a long-lived effect | `rememberUpdatedState` |
| Non-suspend work after every successful composition | `SideEffect` |
| Launch from a callback (a click) | `rememberCoroutineScope()` |

`LaunchedEffect(Unit)` runs once — verify that is genuinely what you want, and that the effect is
not silently stale when a parameter changes.

**Never** mutate state, perform I/O, or read a changing value directly in the composition body.
Composition can run any number of times, in any order, on any thread, and be thrown away.

## Recomposition and stability

- Parameters should be stable: primitives, `String`, immutable `data class` of stable types,
  lambdas that are stable references.
- `List<T>` is **unstable** to the compiler. Use `ImmutableList` (kotlinx.collections.immutable)
  or `@Immutable` on the wrapper.
- Never allocate in the composition body: no `mutableListOf()`, no unremembered object literal —
  it makes a new instance every recomposition and defeats skipping.
- Defer reads to the narrowest scope. Passing `() -> Float` instead of `Float` keeps a frequently
  changing value from recomposing the whole subtree.

## Modifier

- **Every** public composable takes `modifier: Modifier = Modifier` as its **first optional
  parameter**, and applies it to its outermost layout node.
- Order matters and is not commutative: `padding().background()` differs from
  `background().padding()`. Read modifier chains carefully at audit.
- Never pass a modifier down to more than one child.

## Previews

Every reusable composable has a `@Preview`. Previews are documentation that cannot go stale
silently — they stop compiling.

```kotlin
@Preview(name = "Light")
@Preview(name = "Dark", uiMode = UI_MODE_NIGHT_YES)
@Composable
private fun ProfileScreenPreview() {
    AppTheme { ProfileScreen(state = ProfileState(/* sample */), onAction = {}) }
}
```

Preview the **loading, error, and empty** states too — not just the happy path.

## Accessibility

- `contentDescription` on every meaningful image and icon button; `null` for purely decorative.
- Touch targets at least **48 dp**.
- Use `Modifier.semantics { }` to merge or clarify; test with TalkBack, not by reading the code.
- Never rely on colour alone to carry meaning.
